# News Text Classification Using TF-IDF and Logistic Regression

## Project Overview

This project focuses on building a machine learning model to classify news articles into five categories using Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques.

The project is part of **Week 5 – Text Classification** of the AI Field Training program.

The main objective is to convert text into numerical features using TF-IDF, train a Logistic Regression classifier, and evaluate its performance on unseen news articles.

## Dataset

The dataset was prepared during the previous Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) and text preprocessing stage.

**Input file:** `news_preprocessed.csv`

The dataset contains three columns:

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| `Text` | Original news article |
| `Label` | News category (0–4) |
| `normalized_text` | Normalized article text |

The dataset initially contained 2,127 articles after the EDA stage.

During classification preparation, three additional duplicate normalized texts were removed, leaving **2,124 articles**.

## Classification Workflow

### 1. Data Loading and Inspection

- Loaded the preprocessed dataset using Pandas.
- Checked dataset dimensions and missing values.
- Examined class distribution.
- Checked for duplicate and empty normalized texts.
- Removed duplicate normalized texts before splitting the data.

### 2. Feature and Target Selection

The normalized text was selected as the input feature, while the category label was used as the target variable.

- **X:** `normalized_text`
- **y:** `Label`

The classification task contains five classes.

### 3. Train-Test Split

The dataset was divided into training and testing sets using an 80/20 split.

| Dataset | Number of Articles |
|---|---:|
| Training Set | 1,699 |
| Testing Set | 425 |
| Total | 2,124 |

The split used:

- `test_size=0.2`
- `random_state=42`
- `stratify=y`

Stratification was applied to preserve the class distribution across the training and testing sets.

### 4. TF-IDF Feature Extraction

TF-IDF (Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency) was used to convert news articles into numerical features.

The vectorizer was fitted only on the training data to prevent data leakage.

- `fit_transform()` was applied to the training data.
- `transform()` was applied to the testing data.

**TF-IDF Matrix Shapes:**

| Dataset | Shape |
|---|---|
| Training | (1699, 26849) |
| Testing | (425, 26849) |

The vectorizer generated **26,849 text features** from the training vocabulary.

### 5. Model Training

A **Logistic Regression** classifier was trained using the TF-IDF features.

```python
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

model = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)

model.fit(X_train_tfidf, y_train)

y_pred = model.predict(X_test_tfidf)
```

Logistic Regression was selected because it is an efficient and interpretable baseline algorithm for text classification, particularly with high-dimensional sparse features.

### 6. Model Evaluation

The trained model was evaluated using:

- Accuracy
- Macro F1-score
- Weighted F1-score
- Precision and Recall
- Classification Report

## Model Results

The Logistic Regression model achieved the following results on the test set:

| Evaluation Metric | Score |
|---|---:|
| Accuracy | **96.94%** |
| Macro F1-score | **96.94%** |
| Weighted F1-score | **96.93%** |

Out of 425 test articles:

- **412 articles** were correctly classified.
- **13 articles** were incorrectly classified.

### Classification Report

| Class | Precision | Recall | F1-score | Support |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 | 0.99 | 0.93 | 0.96 | 81 |
| 1 | 0.96 | 0.99 | 0.98 | 100 |
| 2 | 0.97 | 0.97 | 0.97 | 69 |
| 3 | 0.99 | 0.97 | 0.98 | 74 |
| 4 | 0.95 | 0.98 | 0.97 | 101 |

## Key Findings

- The model achieved a high classification accuracy of 96.94%.
- Performance was relatively consistent across all five classes.
- Classes 1 and 3 achieved the highest reported F1-scores (0.98).
- Class 0 had the lowest recall (0.93), indicating that some articles belonging to this class were incorrectly classified.
- Macro F1 and Weighted F1 were very close, suggesting relatively balanced performance across categories.
- TF-IDF combined with Logistic Regression provided a strong baseline for news classification.

